## Introduction
Driving the Porsche 911 GT3 Cup in iRacing can be a frustrating experience, especially when you're consistently several seconds off the pace. Even experienced drivers often struggle to find their rhythm in this car. It's unintuitive, lacks information in the force feedback and forces you to rethink everything you love about driving. After spending weeks driving this car competitively, I've finally cracked the code. In this article, I'll share the key insights that will help you adapt to the Porsche 911 GT3 Cup and bring your lap times down. If you're impatient, skip to section 5 as it gives away all the secrets. But to prove that there's method to my madness, I'll explain why my findings make sense.

## 1. Differences to GT3
The Porsche cup has smaller tyres than the GT3, which means less grip and less corner exit. It also has much less aerodynamics than the GT3, making the car less predictable, slower and more difficult to drive. This is further compounded by the fact that the ABS and TC are less sophisticated than the GT3, but similar power. The ABS is basic. These cars didn't always have ABS and they were actually more intuitive to drive without it, albeit slower. Nowadays, then that basic ABS kicks in, especially while steering, you lose a lot of grip and time and the car doesn't really tell you anything about it. And if that's not enough, these cars didn't have Traction Control in the past either, but now they do. The traction control is equally basic and when it kicks in, it's crippling. Spins are unrecoverable and even if you catch the slide, you're going to get swallowed up by the car behind you. If you're used to GT3, you'll be confused and discouraged by the way it punishes you for not knowing its secrets. Luckily this video has the secrets.

## 2. Smooth Driving
Most modern rear wheel drive cars reward a certain amount of smoothness. The Porsche cup is no different, but it's much more sensitive to harsh inputs due to the characteristics we've already covered. The car is often used by professional GT3 drivers to find a driver's bad habits. Knowing ABS/TC limits, rotation amounts, corner speeds braking points, alternative lines, etc are all made exponentially more difficult by the iRacing tyre model. And while I'm not here to complain about iRacing, it's important to understand that the tyre model is a key factor in making this car so difficult to drive. Because once you go over the limit, not only is it difficult to recover the car but the car actually takes several corners in some cases to get back to full grip. And while iRacing extremists blame tyres overheating for this behaviour, it's not a true reflection of how a small slide recovers in real life. So smooth driving is key. 

## 3. Braking
This is where you'll find the biggest difference to the GT3. The Porsche cup cannot rely on complex aerodynamics to get the car pointed in the right direction. If you brake too hard, ABS kicks in and turning turns the tarmac into ice. If you brake too lightly, you'll end up braking way earlier and the People who have already watched this video already will outbrake you and know you're never going to overtake them again because you're likely to just continue overdriving the car. Honestly I felt like I was in jail when I first started driving this car. I'm normally towards the front of the grid but I couldn't even scrape top 20 for weeks. Even when I examined the data on Garage61, I couldn't see how other people were braking the same amount as me but still managing to be faster. And braking is where I found the most time. I'll explain it in a later section, but right now, I have one more topic to cover and it's an important one.

## 4. Balance
As alluded to, the weight is at the rear of the car. It's not mid engined, it's rear engined. So the engine sits right behind the rear wheels.  This means the car is naturally prone to oversteer when off throttle and understeer when on throttle. It makes the car VERY sensitive to weight transfer, really emphasises what we talked about earlier when it comes to smooth driving. However, this alsocomes with some advantages and that's what this video teaches you to exploit. You want to avoid turning while braking. You want to avoid turning while accelerating. Ok that's a bit simplistic. You want to avoid hard braking while turning and you want to avoid hard acceleration while turning. In other words while turning, this car loves a bit of trail braking and a delicate feather of throttle instead of an aggressive lift-off. It pays to know where the balance of the car is. As with every car, it's towards the front while braking and towards the rear while accellerating. That's why subtle inputs are so important for this car. To compound the issue, iracing's force feedback doesn't communicate the car's balance well once you are over the limit. It accurately tells you what the front might feel like in the real car, but it doesn't make up for the lack of feedback at the rear, g-forces, etc. As a result, it become a bit of a memory simulator and driving this car is heavily based on memory. 

## 5. Solution
Ok so what's the solution? Here are the golden rules for driving the Porsche Cup in iRacing:

1. Never brake more than 80%. In fact, many quick people never go higher than 60%. This is very unintuitive. A small bit of ABS in a straight line is fine, but you want to avoid it while turning. 30% brake is the maximum while turning. 70% brake is the sweet spot while going straight.
2. When braking, downshift aggressively and early. Engine braking is your friend. Remember that a rear engined car has MUCH more weight on the rear even when braking hard in a straight line. So where a front engined car gets light at the rear and is prone to rear lockups due to brakes or differentials, the 911 Cup can use this extra weight to its advantage, avoiding diff locking and even rewarding you for a rearward brake bias. Trust me, move the brake bias back 1 or two clicks once the tyres are warm, and downshift as early as possible. That's how the aliens have been outbraking you.
3. Embrace the rotation. You need to use the brake to encourage rotation. Trail braking puts more weight over the front axles and allows the car to turn more easily. Allthewhile a rearward brake bias helps the rear of the car to step out in a controlled fashion. As long as you're on the brake lightly or on the throttle lightly, this car doesn't really spin. It's only when you over commit to either that it spits you out. 
4. Once you've mastered these things, all other driving rules apply and will come naturally. Good lines, use the space, apex correctly, carry speed, avoid ABS and TC where possible, know the limits, etc. This car just tests your discipline. Those who drive LMP3 or V8 Supercars are likely to understand this car and the quirks of iRacing more than the average GT3 driver. 

## Summary
It's all very simple once you know, right? Well I didn't. And it wasn't until community members realised my struggles and gave me pointers that the missing piece of the puzzle clicked. For me, it was the aggressive and early downshift. I was trying to be smooth under braking and changing gear when the car felt balanced just before turn in. Little did I realise, this was compounding my issues. Once I started downshifting early and aggressively, the car felt much more balanced and predictable. Dare I say 'enjoyable'? I think that some people only love the Porsche Cup because they've figured out the secrets and love that others don't know them. However, I think that most drivers simply don't understand why they are quick and others struggle so much. I want this video to be a leveller. I want it to help everyone understand why this car is so difficult and how you can tame it.

